Jenkins multibranch pipeline demo for Maven on Docker agent

This repository contains a Jenkins Pipeline that uses a Docker agent to run Maven tests inside an isolated container. Jenkins Multibranch Pipeline automatically discovers new branches and runs the same build steps per branch.

Files added:
- `Jenkinsfile`: Declarative pipeline for multibranch builds.
- `Dockerfile`: Docker agent image based on Maven + JDK.
- `pom.xml`: Minimal Maven project with JUnit test.
- `src/main/java/com/example/App.java`
- `src/test/java/com/example/AppTest.java`

Setup steps:
1. Create a Git repository in this folder:
   ```powershell
   git init
   git add .
   git commit -m "initial Jenkins multibranch demo"
   ```
2. Push to your Git server and configure a Jenkins Multibranch Pipeline job that points to this repo.
3. Ensure Jenkins has Docker installed and can run Docker agents.

Jenkins pipeline behavior:
- Uses `agent { dockerfile true }` so the build runs inside the repository's Docker image.
- Runs `mvn -B clean test`.
- Publishes JUnit XML results from `target/surefire-reports/*.xml`.
- Archives the test report XML artifacts.

Demo automatic branch pickup:
1. Create a new branch and push it to the remote:
   ```powershell
   git checkout -b feature/auto-branch-demo
   git push -u origin feature/auto-branch-demo
   ```
2. Jenkins Multibranch Pipeline will discover the new branch during branch indexing and schedule a build automatically.
3. When the branch is deleted, Jenkins will remove the branch job automatically after the next indexing run.

Notes:
- No Jenkinsfile changes are required per branch.
- Every branch uses the same Docker-based Maven agent and archives its own build artifacts.
